<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/27/apple-creator-studio-hero-261027-1793110737172-80b12a6d4d.jpg" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p2">For the past week, I’ve been testing betas of the following updates coming to Apple Creator Studio later this month:</p>
<ul id="ul3"><li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/logic-pro-make-music/id1615087040" rel="noopener noreferrer">Logic Pro</a></li>
<li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pixelmator-pro-edit-images/id6746662575" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pixelmator Pro</a></li>
<li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pages-create-documents/id361309726" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pages</a></li>
<li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/numbers-make-spreadsheets/id361304891" rel="noopener noreferrer">Numbers</a></li>
<li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/keynote-design-presentations/id361285480" rel="noopener noreferrer">Keynote</a></li>
</ul><p id="p4">Final Cut Pro is being updated this month, too, but I haven’t tested it.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-64550-pm2x-1793843026577-b5540d85e7.png" alt="Logic Pro's new Liquid Glass design."><p class="image-caption">Logic Pro’s new Liquid Glass design.</p></div>
<p id="p6">I use Logic Pro to edit MacStories’ podcasts, and so far, I’ve edited four episodes using the updated app. For the limited set of Logic’s features that I use for podcast editing, the experience hasn’t changed much. The loops I’ve created over the years are now in the new Unified Browser on the left side of the window, instead of in the panel on the right, but every other aspect of editing an episode is the same as before.</p>
<p id="p7">But even though my editing workflow hasn’t changed much, the design of the app has. Logic Pro has been given a Liquid Glass makeover that emphasizes black and dark gray over its predecessor’s lighter gray treatment. I’m glad Apple decided to update Logic Pro’s look because it had really begun to look dated. The black palette is far more in line with modern professional apps, which makes it nicer to work in.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/27/apple-creator-studio-logic-pro-for-mac-261027-1793110746229-9ac1a30d92.jpg" alt="Some of the new features available to musicians in Logic Pro. Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Some of the new features available to musicians in Logic Pro. Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p9">Now, just because little has changed for podcast editors doesn’t mean there aren’t substantial changes to Logic Pro for musicians. The app has an enhanced stem splitter that can separate drum elements from each other, giving producers greater control. There’s also a new Play Surface that exposes instruments, drum pads, and chord pads for playing music live. The Play Surface and other Logic Pro and MainStage controls can be accessed using Sidecar on an iPad, too.</p>
<p id="p10">The new Unified Browser, which now houses those loops I’ve made, includes other enhancements, too. You can search using natural language queries, drag a loop into the search field to find similar loops, and quickly narrow results with dynamic tag filtering.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-64827-pm2x-1793843201782-9c4c0588aa.png" alt="Logic Pro's Intelligent Assistant helps you learn the app."><p class="image-caption">Logic Pro’s Intelligent Assistant helps you learn the app.</p></div>
<p id="p12">One of my favorite features is Logic Pro’s new Intelligent Assistant, a new beta feature that <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/apple-creator-studio-to-add-motionvfx-ai-assistants-and-mcp-support-in-november/" rel="noopener noreferrer">combines models from Apple and OpenAI</a> to help guide you through the music creation process.</p>
<p id="p13">Apps like Logic Pro are complex, which can make it hard to know what to do when you’re new to the app. One of the first things I did last year with Codex and Claude Code was create a skill that draws on Apple’s extensive support documentation for Logic Pro to help me navigate the app. Now that’s built right into the app itself, allowing you to pose natural language questions and get answers tailored to the app’s features.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-71713-pm2x-1793843273648-59ea259bab.png" alt="The Intelligent Assistant in Pages."><p class="image-caption">The Intelligent Assistant in Pages.</p></div>
<p id="p15">You’ll find similar Intelligent Assistants in Pages, Numbers, and Keynote. Each is great for getting help with how to use the apps, but you can ask general knowledge questions, too. When I asked the Pages Intelligent Assistant what it could tell me about Logic Pro, it went out to the web and returned with a synopsis of the app and links to Apple product pages. That makes the Intelligent Assistant an interesting option when you need to do research alongside the document you’re creating.</p>
<p id="p16">The More button at the top of the Intelligent Assistant lets you report a concern about the results and track your usage. Beneath your usage statistics is a note that monthly usage is increased for a limited time. I don’t believe Apple has said how long that will be and to what degree usage rates will be reduced after the introductory period, but there is already a button to buy more usage for $49.99. How much that buys you is also unclear, though I’m sure we’ll learn more when the apps are released publicly. The Intelligent Assistant can also be turned off in each app’s settings if you prefer not to use it.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-72959-pm2x-1793843334391-e74322f267.png" alt="Some of the videos available now in Content Hub."><p class="image-caption">Some of the videos available now in Content Hub.</p></div>
<p id="p18">Pages, Numbers, and Keynote have added stock video content to their shared Content Hub for the first time. The collection of landscape and portrait videos that range from around eight to 34 seconds long is large and can be incorporated into your documents royalty free along with the Content Hub’s collections of photos, graphics, backgrounds, and more. Creator Studio’s collection of themes and templates for Pages, Numbers, and Keynote has been extended with these updates, too.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-85104-pm2x-1793843516504-7d1679c27c.png" alt="Testing Pages editing in Claude Code using MCP."><p class="image-caption">Testing Pages editing in Claude Code using MCP.</p></div>
<p id="p20">Another big addition is beta MCP support for Pages, Numbers, and Keynote. In my testing, I was impressed to find a very complete set of features accessible via MCP, which runs a local server and uses a shared JavaScript API that adds a layer that’s exclusive to each app. That means, for example, that you could set up a workflow with an agent to generate invoices in Pages, automatically populate fields in Numbers with the details, from Pages and your payment processor or bank, and then generate slides of the data to report on your revenue results periodically.</p>
<p id="p21">The biggest gotcha is that the app you’re controlling with an AI agent must be open and commands must identify the window you’re targeting if more than one is open. I also found the initial MCP setup a little opaque, but not insurmountable with the help of Claude, which walked me through the setup process and created a usage guide.</p>
<p id="p22">Keynote has also added an open file format called Keynote Interchange Format, also known as Keynote X, that’s based on a JSON-based bundle. It’s designed to be more accessible to AI agents, developers, and other tools for automation, integration with third-party tools, and other use cases.</p>
<p id="p23">Pixelmator Pro has been updated, too. Generate Fill is a new tool that lets you select an area and describe a change. For example, you can trace the outline of a person’s shirt and change its color, preserving the shadows and lighting.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-81748-pm2x-1793843621059-18f41056de.png" alt="Original."><p class="image-caption">Original.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/05/cleanshot-2026-11-04-at-81736-pm2x-1793843638225-e7087ed8ea.png" alt="Edited with Generative Fill."><p class="image-caption">Edited with Generative Fill.</p></div>
<p id="p26">In my tests, where I didn’t attempt to be super precise with my selection, it did a good, though imperfect, job changing a shirt from black to red. More impressive was how it preserved the original texture and look of the shirt. Plus, the results can be tweaked to refine some of the imperfections I saw in my testing.</p>
<p id="p27">One of the biggest changes coming to Creator Studio is one I haven’t had a chance to try yet. Final Cut Pro is adding MotionVFX, image generation, editing inside Pixelmator Pro, keyframing improvements, more transcription languages, the ability to disconnect timeline clips, and more. For more on those changes, which Apple has said will be released later this month, be sure to <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/apple-creator-studio-to-add-motionvfx-ai-assistants-and-mcp-support-in-november/" rel="noopener noreferrer">read my story from last week</a>.</p>
<hr id="hr28"><p id="p29">Overall, I’m impressed with the updates to Creator Studio. It’s good to see the collection of apps updated already with meaningful additions that fill many of the gaps that existed. I hope Apple keeps up the pace.</p>
<p id="p30">I’m also glad to see Apple embracing MCP and an open format for Keynote. As AI agent use has risen, I’ve felt the pull of web apps that have extensive APIs and their own MCP integrations, even though I prefer Apple’s Creator Studio apps. As someone who already uses Logic Pro and Pixelmator Pro extensively and Final Cut Pro to a lesser extent, those types of integrations will make it easier to stick with the native apps I like best.</p>
